scsi: ufs: qcom: do not enable the lane clocks twice

ufs_qcom_disable_lane_clks() does nothing when the lane clocks are off,
but ufs_qcom_enable_lane_clks() enabled them again when they were on. A
runtime resume calls it twice, from ufshcd_setup_clocks() (the link is
in hibern8) and from ufs_qcom_resume(), while a suspend switches them
off once, so every runtime suspend/resume cycle left one more enable on
each clock of host->clks, which devm_clk_bulk_get_all() fills with all
the UFS clocks, ref_clk among them. On SM4375 (Xiaomi Redmi Note 12 5G)
the counts climbed with the disk's idle cycles and the XO stayed voted
for in the RPM's sleep set: no XO shutdown, no VDD minimization.

Return early when the lane clocks are already on, as the disable side
does.

"scsi: ufs: ufs-qcom: Enable only lane clocks in lane clock APIs"
(Nitin Rawat, 2026-07) keeps the shared clocks out of these calls, which
stops their leak, but not that of the lane clocks themselves.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c b/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c
index 954be9343..42eb6efd4 100644
--- a/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c
@@ -356,6 +356,9 @@ static int ufs_qcom_enable_lane_clks(struct ufs_qcom_host *host)
 {
 	int err;
 
+	if (host->is_lane_clks_enabled)
+		return 0;
+
 	err = clk_bulk_prepare_enable(host->num_clks, host->clks);
 	if (err)
 		return err;
